            {
                cardId: 'contrast',
                title: 'Contrast',
                subtitle: 'You should know this already',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/contrast.png',
                detail: `<h3>Yep. It's contrast. One of the most well-known and easily understood elements of design there is.</h3><br>
                Contrast is the difference in near elements. For example, the contrast between a person and the sky is big (skin and blue are very different colors),
                but the contrast between a person and a wall (often similar colors) are small.<br><br>
                Similar to balance, it's usually advised to keep contrast low. Though in some designs, contrast is important, like focusing on a target in an image,
                having a design pop out more, etc.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/contrast.png' height=250px><br>
                <b>An example of contrast when it gets too high:</b><br><br>
                <img src='images/contrast_me.jpg' height=250px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'emphasis',
                title: 'Emphasis',
                subtitle: 'When contrast intentionally high',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/emphasis.png',
                detail: `<h3>When contrast is intentionally high to make a part of the image more visible or noticeable.</h3><br>
                Emphasis is best used when you want something to stand out. Have a person you want to highlight? Put a dark background and shine a light on him.
                Have an item you want to contrast? Make the surrounding elements as different as possible, but similar to each other.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/emphasis.jpg', height=350px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'hierarchy',
                title: 'Hierarchy',
                subtitle: `Hey, I'm as confused as you on this`,
                image: 'images/thumbnails/hierarchy.png',
                detail: `<h3>From what I understand of hierarchy, it isnt as complicated as it sounds, it just has a strange name.</h3><br>
                Hierarchy in design is how easily an image is processed. A super complicated image with about 7 different subjects is gonna be
                pretty bad as a logo or something you want the viewer to see and then leave.<br>
                <br>
                The reason it's called "hierarchy" is because it's referring to how 'important' each subject in an image is. So kinda similar to contrast, but 
                a little more important.<br>
                <br>
                It's best used when you have a specific item or subject in an image you want seen first, especially if there's a second you want them to see as well.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/hierarchy.png' height=325px>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'movement',
                title: 'Movement',
                subtitle: `The 'flow' of an image. Don't ask me how an image moves`,
                image: 'images/thumbnails/movement.png',
                detail: `<h3>Movement refers to where the viewer's eye travels over a design.</h3><br>
                If you have a <a data-open-card="principles:hierarchy">hierarchy</a> of subjects in an image, how are you going to control where a viewer's eyes are going to 
                go first? Who's to say they won't look at the second one first? Or the third? Fifth?<br>
                <br>
                This is what movement is about. A design with good 'movement' should have a clear flow of where the subjects are and what to look at first, next, and last.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/movement.png' height=250px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'pattern',
                title: 'Pattern',
                subtitle: 'Use your brain, figure it out',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/pattern.png',
                detail: `<h3>Pattern is just that. A pattern.</h3><br>
                It is literally just a repeated design in an image. That is all a pattern is.<br>
                Seriously. I literally don't have anything more to write here. So example image it is.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/pattern.jpeg' height=350px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'proportion',
                title: 'Proportion',
                subtitle: 'I aint even saying anything',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/proportion.png',
                detail: `<h3>Proportion is literally just how big or small a thing is compared to another thing.<br>
                That's it.</h3><br>
                The larger, the easier it is to see. <a data-open-card='principles:contrast'>Contrasting</a> small things is the best way to
                make the small things higher in the <a data-open-card='principles:hierarchy'>hierarchy</a>.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/proportion.png' height=350px>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'repetition',
                title: 'Repetition',
                subtitle: 'A <i>little</i> bit different than pattern',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/repetition.png',
                detail: `<h3>Repeating an element in an image is a good way to make it very high in the <a data-open-card='principles:hierarchy'>hierarchy</a>.</h3><br>
                Obviously, making a bunch of a thing makes it very easy to see that thing. Horrible when overdone, though; usually used to just 'spice' an image up.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/repetition.png' height=350px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'rhythm',
                title: 'Rhythm',
                subtitle: 'Pattern and movement in one',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/rhythm.png',
                detail: `<h3>Rhythm is <i>how</i> the eyes move rather than where. How cleanly you look from one element to another.</h3><br>
                A good example for this is music. Rhythm in design is the same as rhythm in music.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/rhythm.png' height=350px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'unity',
                title: 'Unity',
                subtitle: 'Make one thing the <i>only</i> thing',
                image: 'images/thumbnails/unity.png',
                detail: `<h2>Make the thing you want the viewer to see the ONLY thing to see.</h2><br>
                If you want something to stand out, a good way is to make it the only thing to be able to stand out. Whether that be one object in the scene or a certain 'vibe',
                you can't ignore something if it's the only thing.<br>
                <br>
                Think of it like this. Say you want a photo of your bedroom. Now, why would you add rocks?<br>
                Answer: you don't. Rocks aren't meant to be in bedrooms. A rock would break the unity of the concept of a bedroom.<br>
                Another way to think of it is like adding salt to fries. Salt is good on fries, it's even expected. That's a complimentary additive to fries. Now, add water.
                Salt and fry soup. Now the unity of fries is ruined and you have a lot of wet fries.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Example:</b><br>
                <img src='images/unity.png' height=250px>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'variety',
                title: 'Variety',
                subtitle: `It's... variety.`,
                image: 'images/thumbnails/variety.png',
                detail: `<h2>Variety is pretty much the opposite of <a data-open-card='principles:unity'>unity</a>.</h2><br>
                Variety is adding elements that don't fit on purpose, but in the right way. Fry soup? Horrible. But I've seen some very interesting recipies. For example,
                marshmallow, buttermilk and chocolate cookies, called <a href='https://shannonmessenger.com/keeper-of-lost-cities/recipes.html'>Mallowmelt cookies</a>.
                They're not your average cookie, but they're amazing.<br>
                <br>
                <b>The cookies:</b><br>
                <img src='images/mallowmelt.png' height=250px><br>
                <br>
                <b>Example of variety:</b><br>
                <img src='images/variety.png' height=250px><br>`
