    {
        //Gifs
        menuId: 'gif',
        title: 'Gifs',
        subtitle: 'All about making a gif and all that junk',
        image: 'icons/loading.gif',
        hidden: true,
        labels: [
            {
                cardId: 'gif:project',
                title: 'Project',
                banner: true,
                subtitle: 'What I did',
                detail: `<h2>If you wanna know how to make a gif, I'm the guy. I know gifs.</h2><br>
                In fact, I'd say I know them too well. I almost always go the manual route when the most simple
                of tools fail.<br>
                <br>
                For example, the most common way I edit a gif is with a site called <a href='https://ezgif.com'>ezgif.com</a>.<br>
                If that fails, or what I want is too complicated of an ask for it, I extract the raw data of the gif in images using the site,
                and manually go through each image one-by-one with <a href='https://www.getpaint.net'>paint.net</a>.<br>
                <br>
                Obviously this is horriffically inefficient. But it (at least somewhat) works.<br>
                <br>
                <h3>Some examples of my (horrible) work:</h3><br>
                <img src='images/sy.gif' height=250px><br>
                The original gif:<br>
                <img src=images/sy_original.gif height=150px><br>
                pretty much all I did was make it blue and got rid of the yellow background. I did it to get an animated pfp, also because this fits
                one of my characters *very* well (named Obake, a shapeshifter character). I got it from a video animation from <a href='https://www.tiktok.com/@znackish'>Znackish</a>.<br>
                <br>
                <h3>Also, I have done some manual gifs (as in animation), but they arent great with my horrible art skills:</h3><br>
                <img src='images/animation.gif' height=250px><br>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'gif:notes',
                title: 'Notes',
                banner: true,
                subtitle: 'A description of what gifs are and how to make them',
                detail: `<h2>So you wanna make a gif?</h2><br>
                First step is to find out what you wanna make.<br>
                Now you need to find what medium to use. Whether it be the most specialized gif tool or simply gluing a bunch of images together,
                what works for you works.<br>
                Then, with whatever tool you have, you simply make your idea.<br>
                <br>
                <h3>It's that simple!</h3><br>
                Seriously. That's literally all you need to make a gif. An idea, a tool, and... that's it.<br>
                <br>
                <h2>Some tools I use (and others):</h2>
                <h2><a href='https://ezgif.com'>Ezgif.com</a></h2>
                A very simple and intuitive tool with many features for gif and video-related editing. Works in browser without need to install anything,
                and (from what I've seen), no ads!<br>
                <h2>Any animation program</h2>
                If it animates, it can gif.<br>
                <h2><a href='https://www.adobe.com/ca/products/photoshop.html'>Photoshop</a></h2>
                I don't use this one, cuz you have to install it, but it's <i>the</i> go-to, so much so that editing an image in any capacity is called 
                'photoshopping' it.`
            }
        ]     
    },



    {
        // Album cover

        menuId: 'albumcover',
        title: 'Album cover',
        subtitle: 'Reload the page and see something neat!',
        image: `${randomAlbumCover[Math.floor(Math.random() * randomAlbumCover.length)]}`,
        hidden: true,
        labels: [
            {
                cardId: 'project',
                title: 'Project',
                banner: true,
                subtitle: 'What I did',
                detail: `<h2>I had a lot of ideas for this project. Though it was easy to settle on one.</h2><br>
                At the start, I was thinking of making a very simple album cover with just a guy and a detective hat, side view of his face. I was thinking
                of I Expect You To Die and it's music, as well as Mission Impossible and one specific song from CG5 which I don't know the name of (and am
                too lazy to search up).<br>
                Then I drifted to another genre and artist I like. In fact, I was thinking of the 
                <a data-open-card='autobio:credits'>creator</a> of this exact site template. Yes, they even make music too!<br>
                Most of their music is very 'electronic', and one song I really like, called <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Gf02GVdI7D0'>Rain</a>.
                Given my propensity for computers and my current attempt of simulating the O(√n) speedup of Grover's algorithm, in Quantum Computers. And of course,
                every new technology needs their mathematical notation, thus the Bra and the Ket was born:<br>
                <img src='images/braket.png'><br>
                Bra and ket were a derivitive from a misspelling of Bracket (braket → bra + ket). A bra maps each vector (quantum bit: qubit) to a number in the 
                complex plane, and a ket maps a vector in complex Vector space of N-1 dimensions (weird probability jargon).<br>
                Pretty much, become a quantum scientist and *then* you can understand this album cover.<br>
                <br>
                <b>Design elements used:<br>
                1. Space<br>
                2. Emphasis<br>
                3. Unity<br>
                <br>
                My project:</b><br>
                <img src='images/albumcover.gif'>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'reflection',
                title: 'Reflection',
                banner: true,
                subtitle: `I'm sure you know what reflection means. Right...?`,
                detail: `<h2>I had a decent bit of fun with this project.<br>
                Reflection questions:</h2><br>
                
                <ol>
                    <li><b>Which design principles did you focus on most?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>Space, definitely. Most of it is white.<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>How do your design choices reflect the genre and mood?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>It doesn't do this horriffically well, but it works amazingly if you know a little bit about quantum mechanics and the such.<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>What was challenging about not using text?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>It wasn't really much of a challenge for me. I'm not the best with text anyway 
                    (I can't even apply any fonts I download! Trust me, I've tried.)<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>What would you call your album based on your product?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>There's many name ideas I have for this cover. So, why not list all of them?<br></li></ul>
                </ol>
                <ul><ul><ol>
                    <li>Ternary → A better version of Binary. It takes the pros of <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVsUOuSjvcg'>analog computers</a> and the pros of 
                    regular binary computers to make the best of both worlds. Yet we don't use it.<br>
                    Source: <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R4achTEgXEw'>The System That Could Replace Binary And Change Computers FOREVER</a><br><br></li>
                    <li>Lightspeed → In refrence to recent quantum computers using <a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rbxcd9gaims'>photons</a><br><br></li>
                    <li>404 → The HTTP code for when the connection to the site failed. There's also 400 → corruption, 403 → access denied (an error a bunch of us got in
                    Social Studies not 30 minutes ago), or even 418, meaning literally, 'I'm a teapot', made by HTTP coders as an april fools joke against brewers.</li><br>
                </ul></ul></ol>
                `
            }
        ]  
    },


    {
        //Final font
        menuId: 'finalfont',
        title: 'Font project',
        image: 'images/digitized.png',
        subtitle: 'Creating a neat design of a font in an object',
        hidden: true,
        labels: [
            {
                cardId: 'partone',
                title: 'Part 1',
                subtitle: 'Creating the image',
                detail: `<h2>I am not an artist.</h2><br>
                Though, my best art in art is usually geometric. A good example of a common drawing of mine is a <a data-open-card='sierpinski'>Sierpinski triangle</a>.
                Besides that, here's what I thought of:
                <ol>
                    <li>A computer with all the parts as words, and each font representing each part</li>
                    <li>A rubik's cube with each face as a part of 'rubik's cube' (rub, iks, cube)</li>
                </ol>
                And that's where my brain went dry, so I just went with the cube.<br>
                <br>
                My first order of buisness was to draw the cube lightly in pencil, to get the frame. And that's what I did, with a trace image (overlayed here)<br><br>
                <img src='images/rubiksframe.png'><br>
                After that, I struggled. The program I was using, paint.net, didn't allow stretching images (called "liquify"), and the only way to do that in the program 
                was to install addons to paint.net. It required administrator to do that.<br>
                To counteract this, I simply made three faces of the cube, and searched online for a net-to-cube program. Luckily, 
                <a href='https://thecube.ninja/'>I found one</a>.<br>
                Ignoring all the ads and popups to install viruses and/or bloatware (be careful!), I managed to create this:<br><br>
                <img src='images/netofacube.png'><br><br>
                Stenciling the same way I did for the last one, I drew all of the letters carefully, and ended up with this:<br><br>
                <img src='images/uncolored.png'><br><br>
                And eventually, after tracing with marker and coloring, I had this:<br><br>
                <img src='images/colored.png'>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'parttwo',
                title: 'Part 2',
                subtitle: 'Porting to the digital world',
                detail: `<h2>I have a confession to make.</h2>
                For all the previous projects, I've had a workaround to not use Adobe Illustrator. For the <a data-open-card='gif'>Gif project</a>, I already had some gifs made
                using <a href='https://ezgif.com'>Ezgif.com</a>. For the Album Cover project, all I needed was paint.net and the same <a href='https://ezgif.com'>Ezgif.com</a>
                to do that.<br>
                In fact, I havent even installed Adobe Illustrator yet (currently installing as I write this, as a matter of fact!). This'll be my test, to see how good
                I am at everything computers.<br><br>
                
                <b>Update: My hatred for Adobe only gets worse by the day.</b><br>
                No more time in the day to finish anything else, because I waited 30 minutes with my hand up, severely confused. Couldn't group the selections; 
                "Can't make a group of objects that are within different groups", despite never making a group prior. Couldn't select something; the solution was to 
                accidentally press P after 30 minutes and use the brush tool to draw in the red.<br>
                Here's what I got on Illustrator after I pretty much gave up:<br>
                <br>
                <img src='images/ihateillustrator.png'><br><br>
                Now using paint.net:<br><br>
                <img src='images/digitized.png'>`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'partthree',
                title: 'Part 3',
                subtitle: 'Reflection',
                detail: `<h2>Guess who hates Adobe!</h2>
                <a data-open-card='autobio'>This guy</a> hates Adobe!<br><br>
                But seriously, this kind of shows how good I am at computers. I haven't tried the easy way because I've gotten so used to the hard, which makes me 
                incredibly good at the hard.<br><br><br><br>

                <h3>Concept and Design:</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><b>What word did you choose and why?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>I like puzzles. I like Rubik's cubes. Do I need more reason?<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>How did you turn it into a visual image?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>I'm good at drawing shapes and straight lines. A Rubik's cube to fit the word "Rubiks Cube" simply makes sense.<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>Why did you choose your final colors?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>The word I chose keeps answering these questions for me. My idea that took 2 minutes to come up with is apparently the best idea there is.<br>
                    But for specific colors, I just used what I had (I had those color pencil crayons and paint.net has those colors built-in)<br><br></li></ul>
                </ol>
                <br><br>
                <h3>Evaluation:</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li><b>What is the strongest part of your logo?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>I guess the E? It's contrasted because it's sideways. Either that or the nice movement.<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>What would you improve?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>It's very... bland? Like, most Typography designs use rounded objects for a reason; to exaggerate the text in interesting ways. 
                    Mine just doesn't have that.<br><br></li></ul>

                    <li><b>How does your logo show strong design principles (simplicity, readability, limited colors)?<br><br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>It's very simple. It's an unscrambled Rubik's cube, the definition of simple. There's no puzzle because the puzzle is solved.<br>
                    It's also fairly readable and only uses 3 colors (4 if you count black as one of the colors). Also, it has amazing movement to it.<br><br></li></ul>
                </ol>
                `
            }
        ]
    },
    {
