            }
        ]
    },

    {
        //Satirical logo Design
        menuId: 'editedlogo',
        title: 'Logo',
        subtitle: 'The logo project? What else would this be?',
        image: 'images/thumbnails/archbtw.png',
        hidden: true,
        labels: [
            {
                cardId: 'companyresearch',
                title: 'Company Research',
                subtitle: `It's the company research`,
                banner: true,
                detail: `<h3>Turns out I went overboard. Like usual.</h3>
                I was <i>supposed</i> to only do one company, and I was also supposed to the research <i>before</i> I made the logo. Yet here we are:<br>
                <br>
                <img src='images/archbtw.png'><br>
                So I guess I'm doing two companies!<br>
                <br>
                <h2>First company: Microslop</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li><b>Name: Microsoft<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Founding date: April 4th, 1975<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Founded by: Bill Gates & Paul Allen<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Provided Serivces:<br></b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Beginning: BASIC interpreter for the MITS microcomputer<br></li>
                        <li>Middle: Started making OSes in 1980 with Unix (a public base for almost every operating system)<br></li>
                        <li>Today: Has made several technological developments alongside selling the top OS in the world (created a new state of matter!)<br></li>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>Mission statement:<br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>from Microsoft's website, "We believe that companies that can do more, should"<br></li></ul><br>
                    <li><b>target audience:</b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Gamers<br></li>
                        <li>Developers<br></li>
                        <li>Almost everyone with a computer<br></li>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>Brand's visual elements:</b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Logo: Very recognizable and very simple "window" logo, simply just four squares.<br></li>
                        <li>Color: Red, green, blue, and yellow. All the primary colors, including additive (red, green, blue, like computer screens) and subtractive 
                        (red, yellow, blue, like in art). Also, the text is a distinct and recognizable gray<br></li>
                        <li>Font: <a style="font-family: 'Segoe UI semibold', sans-serif;">Segoe UI semibold</a>; very simple and recognizable font<br></li>
                        <li>Style: Incredibly simplistic. 'The basic'.<br></li>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>What image does the company try to present:</b></li>
                    <ul><li>Microsoft tries to present itself as best as possible. It tries it's hardest to create new and amazing features, and praises them even if they're
                    bad. Microsoft wants people to think it's a tool of need instead of want.<br></li></ul><br>
                    <li><b>What are some criticisms, controversies, or public opinions about the company?<br>
                    Ex. Labour practices, Environmental issues, Marketing tactics, Health concerns, Cultural impact:<br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>Many people think Microsoft is extremely invasive and does whatever it wants without regard for what you want. It assumes what you want, often
                    when you don't want it.<br>
                    For example, there is no straightforward way to remove Copilot 365 from the Taskbar on Windows 11. The way I did it was through a tech Youtuber's bloatware
                    removal tool. There is a LOT of bloat on Windows compared to other OSes; the OS I mainly use, Linux (KDE Neon), is barely two Gigabytes when installed on a USB 
                    drive. Windows is seven. And that's just the installer; the actual OS size is much worse. For Linux, a decent chunk of what is installed is already on the drive,
                    and most of the space taken up of the drive is empty space needed for other systems. For windows, it needs an internet connection to install, meaning that 
                    7 gigabytes is JUST the install script, not what is actually being installed.<br></li></ul><br>
                    <li><b>Based on your research, what do you think the brand actually represents?<br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>I think it represents bloatware. A child, upset when you don't like it's drawing, forcing you to look at it and put it on the fridge.
                    It represents how companies don't care about the audience and just care about their wants. They don't care that you don't like Gemini, they like it so you must
                    like it as well.</li></ul>
                </ol>
                <i>Sources: <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft'>Wikipedia</a>, <a href='https://www.microsoft.com/en-ca/about'>Microsoft Website</a></i>
                <br>
                <br>
                <br>
                <br>
                <h2>Second company: I use Arch btw</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li><b>Name: Linux<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Founding date: September 17th, 1991<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Founded by: Linux Torvalds<br></b></li>

                    <li><b>Provided Serivces: <br></b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>An open-source and completely free kernel<br></li>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Note: A kernel is the second layer in a computer's infrastructure. It connects the user interface to the hardware.<br>
                                Example: A rasberry pi; the hardware is on the board and the software is written into through a computer. But the kernel interprets that software
                                and implements it to the components</li>
                        </ul>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>Mission statement:<br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>From Linux's website, "Empowering the generation of open source innovators"<br></li></ul><br>
                    <li><b>target audience:</b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Developers and coders<br></li>
                        <li>Software engineers<br></li>
                        <li>Server administrators/technicians<br></li>
                        <li>Companies who don't want to pay and/or create their own kernel (eg. Android)</li>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>Brand's visual elements:</b></li>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Logo: Easily recognizable penguin. You rarely see it, but the few times you do, you can recognize it. Though you usually ignore it
                        since barely anyone uses linux as an OS (I do!!!)<br></li>
                        <li>Color: Simple black and white with a hint of yellow. The black and white are background and secondary, and the yellow is contrasted.<br></li>
                        <li>Font: <a style="font-family: Georgia semibold, serif;">Georgia;</a> a simple font that isn't very commonly used in logos, making it stand out.<br></li>
                        <li>Style: Very distinct; hard to mistake for something else, very easy to recognize even when you don't see it often.<br></li>
                    </ul><br>
                    <li><b>What image does the company try to present:</b></li>
                    <ul><li>It doesn't really need to 'present' itself as anything because it is exactly what they want to present. It's an open-source kernel; ANYONE
                    can use it for free at no cost. ANYONE can make whatever they want with it. ANYONE can make ANYTHING.<br></li></ul><br>
                    <li><b>What are some criticisms, controversies, or public opinions about the company?<br>
                    Ex. Labour practices, Environmental issues, Marketing tactics, Health concerns, Cultural impact:<br></b></li>
                    <ul><li>Being open-source, it's not managed in the slightest. Whatever is made is made by random people, usually at no benefit to themselves.
                    Thus, it's prone to bugs and viruses. In fact, very recently, a trojan was almost implemented across two very core Linux distributions; Debian and
                    RHEL (Red Hat Enterprise Linux). Debian is a fairly popular Linux distro for consumer use, and RHEL is a paid distribution used in governmental
                    services that require upmost secrecy. The attack was on a package manager called XZ, where they discretely rewrote the code of a backdoor called systemd,
                    which gave the user full access to the system if verified. The trojan rewrote the verification code when booting the OS, allowing a backdoor to be
                    accessed, and giving anyone who knew the backdoor access to the entire system. Government files, people's personal data, even important systems like entire
                    <b>power grids</b> were under threat. Though the trojan was caught before it was put onto the OS.<br></li></ul>
                    <li><b>Based on your research, what do you think the brand actually represents?<br></b></li><br>
                    <ul><li>I think it's true to it's word. It's the most used OS in all tech in the world; there's no need to paint their OS as 'better' or something,
                    all they need to do is let the community code their masterpieces.</li></ul>
                </ol>
                <i>Sources: <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux#'>Wikipedia</a>, <a href='https://www.linuxfoundation.org/about'>Linux Foundation`
            },
            {
                cardId: 'redesign',
                title: 'Logo Redesign',
                subtitle: `It's the logo redesign. What else would it be?`,
                banner: true,
                detail: `<h3>I already finished this without doing the <a data-open-card='editedlogo:companyresearch'>Company Resarch</a>, soo...</h3>
                Here's the image:<br>
                <br>
                <img src='images/archbtw.png'><br><br>
                <h2>Explanation:</h2>
                I need something more than just "here's the image" for this section, so here I did my best to reverse-engineer my design choices.
                <h3>Microslop:</h3>
                Given Microsoft's propensity for overexaggerating stuff no matter how bad it is, this term fits. Appending "slop" to something means it's
                something low-quality that is repeatedly given, demanded, or forced. <br>For example, games like Lethal Company and REPO are called 'friendslop'
                as they maximize your experience with friends all the time. Playing these games without friends can be boring and even harder than with. Thus
                you only really play these games when with friends, meaning it repeatedly demands and/or gives friend experiences.<br>
                This fits Windows perfectly. It forces you to use their features no matter how low-quality they may be. Thus, Microslop was born.
                <h3>Arch btw:</h3>
                Linux isn't really a defined 'company'. It's more of a utility. Thus, the thing that defines Linux is not the company, but the community who make
                the distributions.<br>
                A meme in the community is "I use Arch btw". Arch is a distribution of Linux mainly for people with coding expertise (much more coding expertise than me!).
                Arch is the peak of customization; it will let you do whatever you want to it with little warning. In fact, the first time I tried Linux, it was on Arch.
                Specifically a desktop environment called 'CaelestiaOS'. It was a very pretty OS with an automatic tiler called Hyprland that improves workflow. After several
                issues involving the OS thinking my PC was a laptop (showing a battery icon, showing a 'No Wifi' icon when connected via ethernet, etc.), so I switched
                to a much more user-friendly os called KDE Neon. Like all Linux distributions, it's good for coders and developers. It's also one of the best consumer distributions
                out there, behind Ubuntu. It's had it's fair share of issues (GRUB boot manager not working, OBS <i>literally DELETING my GPU</i> (not joking here! Ask me about it!),
                and some other various issues), but it's worked very well in most games and programs.<br>
                Back to Arch, a big meme in the community is that, given how experienced of a coder you have to be to use it effectively, you have to be really smart to be able to
                use it. So using Arch is somewhat of a flex, like "Hey! I can do this really hard thing!". This caused many Arch users to mention that they use Arch even when it's
                not a point in the conversation. Thus, 'I use Arch btw' was born.`
            }
        ]
    },

